  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 223 PM EDT Thu Sep 17 2026\n\nToday and Tonight... \n\nA combination of a strong subgeostrophic upper \njet and weak low pressure over the Atlantic has subdued the \namplitude and strength of a ridge over the SE CONUS, with the upper \njet now dipping into portions of the Ohio Valley. This has been the \nfocus for mutliple round of convection over the last 24 hours, the \nlast of which as produced a outflow boundary currently pushing \nthrough central Indiana. Locations south of the boundary will \ncontinue to see typical diurnal curves with temperatures in the low \nto mid 90s. North of the boundary, temperatures have dropped 5 to 10 \ndegrees. As the boudnary mixes this afternoon, temperatures will \nattempt to rebound, but likely will still remain at or below 90. \n\nThis strong outflow boundary has produced a multidue of caveats to \nthe forecast leading to fairly high uncertainty in the QPF forecast \njust 12 to 24 hours out. Model guidance has had a difficult time \nassimilating this boundary, of which will likely play a large factor \non where convection is focused late this evening through tonight. \nThere is alos a broader synoptic front that will near from the NE \ntonight. This will likely play out in one of two ways: \n\n    1. The mesoscale boundary and synoptic front are misaligned \nleading to a more diffuse and irregular area of showers and \nthunderstorms. Within this scenario, centtral Indiana would likely \nget a broad area of 0.5-1.0\" of rain with isolated pockets of higher \namounts\n\n    2. The mesoscale boundary and synoptic front align prior to \ninitiation. This would likely result in a more prolific, but narrow \nswath of heavy rain with amounts in the 2-3\" range where the boudary \nsettles. Due to this potential scenario, a Flood Watch has been \nissued from 10PM until 10AM EDT for a majority of East Central \nIndiana. \n\n\nFriday through Monday... \n\nAs mentioned, a synoptic boundary will work its way into central \nIndiana tonight into tomorrow, with a large theta-e gradient across \nthe state tomorrow. This gradient will generally be aligned just \nsouth of the I-74 corridor with temperatures in the low 80s to the \nN/E and upper 80s to the S/W. Synotpic forcing will not be abundant \ntomorrow, and the 800-700mb lapse rates look to be rather weak. That \nsaid, a few isolated showers and storms cannot be ruled out \nalong the boundary in the afternoon. \n\nThe remainder of the weekend will be a constant push/pull of this \nlingering stationary boundary over the region creating both a swing \nin temperatures and persistenant rain chances. The best chances for \nrain look to be Friday night into Saturday as the front shifts back \nto the east. With additional chances late Saturday through Monday \nmorning as weak waves propegate along the boundary. Temperatures are \nexpected to remain warm, with some uncertainty depending on boundary \nplacement and cloud cover extent; general expectations are for highs \nin the upper 80s each afternoon for Indianapolis.",
